HMRC places limits on the amount of tax-free benefits that can be taken from pension schemes. Theses are called the lump sum allowance and the lump sum & death benefit allowance. The limits apply to benefits during your lifetime and on death. Income tax is payable on benefits taken above these amounts. Your remaining allowances reduce each time you take benefits. Your personal allowances may be higher than the standard amounts if you've been granted one or more of the types of protections by HMRC.
