cies":["The liquidation order in the proposed scenario schedules the £6,000 Savings account as 'LIQUIDATED 2ND', meaning it is designated to be drawn upon if needed for cashflow shortfalls, contrary to typical emergency fund preservation.","The report asserts emergency funds are retained but does not quantify what constitutes an adequate emergency fund or state whether £6,000 is sufficient for the clients' circumstances.","The report identifies liquidity needs funded from 'Cash savings' and 'Pension arrangements via Flexi Access Drawdown' but does not explain the interaction between these two or prioritise access to the cash emergency fund versus pension drawdown."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":[],"analysis":"The evidence does not show the emergency fund being depleted during the modelled years (2025-2056), as the cashflow surplus/deficit remains at £0 throughout and liquid assets decline only marginally (from £6,000 to approximately £5,900 over 2-3 years) [P4, P10, P12]. However, the comparative analysis reveals material concerns about emergency fund appropriateness. First, the liquidation order explicitly places the £6,000 savings account second in the queue for drawdown [P1], indicating it is not being ring-fenced but designated as available to cover income shortfalls. Second, the report does not quantify an emergency fund requirement or state why £6,000 is adequate for a couple with combined regular expenditure of approximately £20,616 p/a [P10]. Industry guidance typically recommends 3-6 months of expenses; £6,000 represents only 3.5 months of stated expenditure, and this is not addressed in the report. Third, the report's assertion that liquidity needs are 'funded from Cash savings' is vague about sequencing and does not clarify whether the cash will be preserved as true emergency cover or treated as available working capital. The proposed scenario shows the Surplus Account starting at £0 [P1, P13], meaning the £6,000 savings are the first actual liquid buffer; their designation as 'LIQUIDATED 2ND' (after an empty surplus account) in the liquidation order indicates they are the primary source of emergency drawdown. While the modelled cashflow does not force this drawing during normal years, the structure places emergency reserves at material risk if income or expenditure assumptions deteriorate. The guard 'A cashflow that schedules the emergency fund to be spent has not retained it, whatever the report says about retaining liquid capital' applies: the liquidation order demonstrates the £6,000 is scheduled as an available drawdown source, not protected. The report makes no case for why £6,000 is adequate or how it will be preserved separate from income needs.","citations":[{"passageId":"P1","source":"intelliflo planning proposed scenario.md","category":"F","quote":null,"cells":["Savings","£6,000","LIQUIDATED 2ND","Cash Account","1.25% expected return p/a"]},{"passageId":"P10","source":"intelliflo planning proposed scenario.md","category":"F","quote":"Currently £1,200 p/a (discretionary) increasing by 2% p/a; Currently £15,660 p/a (essential) increasing by 2% p/a; Currently £1,656 p/a (essential) increasing by 2% p/a; Currently £300 p/a (essential) increasing by 2% p/a; Currently £1,800 p/a (essential) increasing by 2% p/a","cells":["TOTAL REGULAR EXPENSES","£20,616"]},{"passageId":"P12","source":"intelliflo planning proposed scenario.md","category":"F","quote":null,"cells":["2034","78 / 74","138,235","25,023","20,788","0"]},{"passageId":"P9","source":"intelliflo planning Baseline scenario.md","category":"F","quote":null,"cells":["Savings","JS","Cash Account","6,000"]},{"passageId":"P4","source":"intelliflo planning Baseline scenario.md","category":"F","quote":null,"cells":["2027","71 / 67","122,985","24,181","20,620","0"]}],"issueCategories":["Weak justification","Missing evidence"],"severity":"Moderate","outcome":"PotentialConcern"}

