rformance figure, when reversed at the stated basis, implies a fund value of £110,188.68, which does not match any stated plan value (neither the £110,185 stated as the solution total nor the £116,998.47 existing arrangements total).","The -0.24% saving figure when applied as a percentage charge difference implies a fund value of £110,184.09, close to but not exactly matching the stated solution total of £110,185. The small discrepancy could be rounding, but the two competing cost figures (one stating a saving, one stating a 4.24% cost increase) cannot both apply to the same fund value without additional breakdown of what is included in each calculation.","The report does not itemise what adviser fee amount was assumed in the 4.24% calculation, making it impossible to verify whether the difference between -0.24% and 4.24% (a 4.48 percentage point gap) represents a plausible adviser fee rate or whether the calculations rest on different fund values or different charge bases."],"comparisonPerformed":true,"missingInputs":["The adviser fee amount (initial and ongoing components separately) that was assumed in deriving the 4.24% outperformance requirement.","Breakdown of which specific charges were included in the -0.24% calculation versus the 4.24% calculation, since both purport to compare the recommended plan to existing arrangements but yield opposite conclusions about cost direction.","The fund values used as the denominator for each cost figure — the 4.24% and -0.24% percentages imply different fund values when reversed, and neither matches the stated solution total cleanly."],"analysis":"The requirement tests whether a higher-cost recommendation is justified by stated benefits. The report asserts two contradictory facts: that the plan is cheaper (by 0.24%) when measured by annual recurring charges alone, and that it is more expensive (by 4.24%) when adviser fees are included. The guard permits higher cost where benefits justify it; however, the precondition is that the cost comparison itself must be sound. The two figures cannot both be correct of the same recommendation. When the 4.24% is reversed arithmetically (dividing by 0.0424), it implies a fund value of approximately £110,189, and when -0.24% is reversed, it implies £110,184. Both are close to the stated £110,185 solution total, but the discrepancy between them (a 4.48 percentage point gap, equivalent to roughly £4,672 in annual charges on £110,185) is not explained. The report does not state what adviser fee rate or amount was baked into the 4.24% figure. This makes it impossible to verify whether the cost increase is real, what it comprises, or whether the stated benefits (which the report does not explicitly itemise in relation to this cost increase) actually justify it. The report claims both that charges fall and that the plan requires 4.24% outperformance — a logical impossibility unless the bases differ radically. Since the bases are stated as 'annual recurring charges' (for -0.24%) and 'including adviser fee' (for 4.24%), and no adviser fee rate is disclosed, the comparison cannot be verified as sound. This is not a matter of evidence absence; it is internal incoherence within the report's own assertions.","citations":[{"passageId":"I_RecommendationsAndAdvice_page_11_cost_1","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.pdf, page 11","category":"I_RecommendationsAndAdvice","quote":"Based on information supplied by your existing providers, our calculations show that the Aviva plan is 0.24% less expensive than your current plans.","cells":null},{"passageId":"I_RecommendationsAndAdvice_page_11_cost_2","source":"Suitability Report Test 1.pdf, page 11","category":"I_RecommendationsAndAdvice","quote":"As we have agreed to be remunerated via the policy (initial and ongoing), our calculations showed that the Aviva plan is 4.24% more expensive than your current plans.","cells":null}],"issueCategories":["Data inconsistency","Weak justification"],"severity":"High","outcome":"Pote