iterature [P8] confirms 'An auto-switching investment approach is a way of investing your client's money over time, where it moves to different funds as your client moves closer to retirement' and the fund is 'Aviva Pensions My Future Focus Consolidation (Pre-2024) S2' showing it uses lifestaging. The new Aviva solution [P22] has platform charge 0.21% and investment management 0.22% totalling 0.44%. On alternatives: file notes [P16-P17] show alternatives were considered (People's Pension 'discounted as no servicing and reduced flexibility', Aviva alternative 'only mixed asset aviva fund available is risk rated 4 which does not meet the clients ATR', Zurich alternative rejected because 'no multi asset funds in his risk range', Standard Life rejected on cost grounds). On Scottish Widows encashment: the plan value is £211.32 [P21], but the report's small pots example uses £200, and calculates net proceeds as £170 (80.95% of £211.32, not 80% of £200 as the narrative suggests). Scottish Widows information [P14] confirms 'no guarantees, discounts or bonuses attached to this member's plan' and 'Money4Life Plans do not support ongoing advisor charging'.","discrepancies":["Report asserts 'lower costs' as primary switch benefit, but Pension Switch Charges table shows Standard Life impact +0.26% (new plan MORE expensive); Aviva comparison states 'plan itself is 0.06% more expensive' which contradicts 'lower costs' claim","Report states small pots example uses £200 with £170 net proceeds, but actual plan value is £211.32; the calculation basis and projected proceeds are misaligned","File notes discount Zurich alternative on grounds that 'blend of funds have not performed as well as what we are offering on Aviva marginally', yet report does not disclose this performance comparison or explain why Zurich was rejected in final recommendation narrative","Report recommends consolidation to Aviva Platform but file notes state Zurich alternative fund blend 'have not performed as well',